there was a very interesting shift that  happened right at the end of 2019 which  I believe is now going to dictate a lot  of the narrative around the 2020 primary  for the first quarter of 2020 certainly  into and through the first few primaries  and the shift is that both Democratic  Party insiders and even some in  corporate media seem to have had like a  light bulb go off where they realized  wait a second Bernie Sanders might  actually win the Democratic nomination  now this is not an analysis of whether  Bernie could be Trump we've done that  analysis that's a separate story this is  not an analysis of Bernie's policy this  is not an analysis of whether Bernie  Sanders is a communist he's not we've  done those analyses this is about  something else  we've talked about the Bernie blackout  that took place during much of 2019 in  terms of covering Bernie Sanders  campaign in a serious manner by  corporate media and we've also talked  about the attitude of some in the DNC  that Bernie simply can't win others on  the other hand believe that Bernie could  win and were scared so they were meeting  about how to try to stop Bernie famously  Pete Budaj edge was even included in  some of those early stop Bernie sort of  meetings that were taking place about  how can we make sure that we deny Bernie  Sanders the nomination but in the last  few weeks of 2019 things changed we'll  talk about the fundraising piece in a  little bit but a few other things went  on number one  part of it was Elizabeth Warren's  collapse in the polls and she had nearly  tied to Joe Biden for first place in the  Democratic primary a few months ago she  has been on the decline since then she's  dropped to third place she's lost almost  half of her support from her peak a few  months ago much more solidly behind  Bernie more than four points behind as  of this morning now another part of it  is not just Elizabeth Warren's decline  what we've seen if you look at polling  is that many candidates were up and down  and sometimes eventually out while  Bernie Sanders on the other hand has  basically been steady for months and  months  other than some very small shifts so  think back Biden had that huge spike  when he formally announced up to 40%  then he dropped he leveled out every  debate he seems to lose some support  because his performances have been by  and large terrible and then gained some  of it back very erratic okay Elizabeth  Warren climbed as I mentioned all the  way up to almost hi Joe Biden we really  within the margin of error to tie Joe  Biden and then just started sinking  losing almost half of her support Kamala  Harris she climbed all the way up to 15  after one debate in which she had one of  these kill shots against excuse me  punching my microphone against Joe Biden  but then she relic relatively quickly  fell she ended her campaign a couple of  weeks ago as we told you  Julian Castro just announced that he's  ending his campaign Budaj edge had an  upswing didn't really seem believable to  many people and he has settled back down  basically to where he was and this  entire time Bernie Sanders has been  holding steady even his heart attack  only a relatively small bump now there's  two ways to interpret that and I have no  interest in pulling the wool over your  eyes one way to interpret that is that  Bernie Sanders has a very solid base of  support  from which to build and now the question  is how does he build it and we'll talk  about that the other way to think about  this is that Bernie has basically maxed  out that Bernie has hit his ceiling and  barring some very unlikely circumstances  it's great for Bernie that he's held  roughly the same level of support for a  while but he's incapable of growing that  support those are two different opinions  about this and we'll talk about that now  just before the new year  you saw Democratic officials party  operatives pundits all starting to take  Bernie Sanders more seriously David  Brock is a big Democratic insider he was  a Hillary Clinton Ally in 2016 he ran a  pro Hillary PAC in 2016 I was on a panel  with him at Politico back in 2018 he  said quote it may have been inevitable  that eventually you would have two  candidates representing each side of the  ideological divide in the party a lot of  smart people I've talked to lately think  there's a very good chance  these two end up being Biden and Sanders  Sanders now there is no denying that  Bernie Sanders is solidly not in first  place the more I look into things the  more I believe the polls about Joe Biden  and that Joe Biden is solidly in first  place I know that there are a lot of  people in my audience who don't want to  acknowledge that what I have been  increasingly realizing and a call we got  last week or earlier this week actually  solidified that some of us are so  enmeshed in online politics where we say  where are these Biden supporters I'm not  coming across any of these Biden  supporters it feels like we rarely come  across them remember that call I got  explaining for a viewer explain that at  Christmas all of their relatives are  completely in for Biden they are not  voters that are part of the online world  that we exist in and I believe this  applies to millions of Americans so I do  believe Joe Biden is in first place I am  increasingly thinking about how could  the Bernie Warren support be  consolidated because I do believe that  if it could Joe Biden would no longer be  the frontrunner but we'll be talking  more about that as far as today's  conversation Bernie Sanders solidly in  second place being taken more and more  seriously by the establishment and by  corporate media the question now is what  can push Bernie Sanders over the top if  anything it seems as though Elizabeth  Warren going by the polls does not have  what it takes to go the distance her  support eroding by almost 50% over the  last few months the debates aren't going  to help Bernie I mean it just it we've  had a bunch now it's not moving Bernie  support I know that I still have people  in my audience who say David at the next  debate  if Bernie does X if Bernie does Y he  will take support from Biden Warren  whoever it's not going to happen in that  way unless someone seriously blunders  during an upcoming debate someone who  has significant support being Biden  Warren maybe Buddha judge but the  further in you get the less debates move  the needle unless someone really  blunders there is a bigger chance  that Bernie blunders and loses support  then there is that in the debates bernie  overtakes Joe Biden that's not about  Bernie okay I understand that this is  just the way the debate debates work the  further and you get the more candidates  are known the more their positions are  known the the smaller the possibility of  making large gains from the debates  becomes so it becomes a mathematical  question it's also a political question  how can support move to Bernie if that's  where you want to see the support move  what can you do I don't know the answer  Warren falling doesn't seem to help  she's lost almost half her support in  the last few months and Bernie's  basically even let me know your thoughts  what could change the balance towards  Bernie from where we are today a big  piece of evaluating how a candidate is  doing is fundraising in our current  system and I want to talk about that  next do you feel like you just don't  have enough time to become as  intellectually well-rounded as you would  like to be that there are just not  enough hours in your week for you to  read all of the books that you want to  read one of our sponsors is called  blinka stand it's an app that lets you  consume an entire nonfiction book in 15  minutes it's only about eight bucks a  month but you can try it for free for a  week and get 25% off a subscription when  you go to blankest comm slash Pakman  what blink is does is take thousands of  popular nonfiction books and condense  them into either text or audio that you  can read or listen to in just 15 minutes  and what you get is all of the most  important ideas and information from the  book imagine being able to listen to an  entire nonfiction book in the amount of  time it takes you to drive or walk home  from work  I've just finished listening to upheaval  by Jared Diamond Jared Diamond a  tremendous author whose work I've  previously recommended excellent book  looking at some of modern history's  biggest national crises to figure out  how different countries ended up in such  precarious situations  blinkie's has totally transformed how I  consume information I love it of course  I read a book in its entirety when I can  but there are simply just so many books  that I want to read and would not have  the time for if it weren't for bling  cast they have books on politics I can  comics philosophy science you name it  you can try it out yourself totally free  for a week and get 25% off if you decide  to subscribe just by going to blink as  comm slash Pakman I've put the link  right underneath this video

